UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 2: Welcoming newcomers', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Welcoming newcomers means making people feel safe and included. Actions include greeting them by their name and learning how to say it, offering a buddy, showing them around, inviting them to play, using pictures or translation to help and being patient.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich are welcoming actions: asking how to say a new student''s name and practising it; ignoring them until they speak English; inviting them to join a game?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAsking how to say their name and practising it, and inviting them to join a game. Ignoring them is unwelcoming.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a newcomer must learn everything alone leaves out the many ways the community can help.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a welcome guide for a new student with six items, including a simple school map and buddy roles. Write two welcoming sentences and one thing a newcomer could teach the class.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Welcoming newcomers means making people feel safe and included. Actions include greeting them by their name and learning how to say it, offering a buddy, showing them around, inviting them to play, using pictures or translation to help and being patient.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a welcome guide for a new student with six items, including a simple school map and buddy roles. Write two welcoming sentences and one thing a newcomer could teach the class. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Asking how to say their name and practising it, and inviting them to join a game. Ignoring them is unwelcoming. Reasoning: I used welcome by attending to newcomer and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking a newcomer must learn everything alone leaves out the many ways the community can help. Correction: Asking how to say their name and practising it, and inviting them to join a game. Ignoring them is unwelcoming. Why: the correction restores accurate use of welcome.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a newcomer must learn everything alone leaves out the many ways the community can help. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating welcome on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with welcome, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Asking how to say their name and practising it, and inviting them to join a game. Ignoring them is 